Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.870
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.870 --> 00:00:09.600
MARK MANDEL: The term
multiplayer games

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:12.010
is wide and varied.

00:00:12.010 --> 00:00:14.430
We want to talk about very
specific types of games--

00:00:14.430 --> 00:00:16.170
sort of put some
constraints on it.

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:18.780
So the types of multiplayer
games we want to talk about

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:23.650
are your fast-paced, online,
competitive, multiplayer games.

00:00:23.650 --> 00:00:25.560
Right, for example,
your Overwatchs

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:28.650
or your Fortnites or your Rocket
Leagues-- things like that.

00:00:28.650 --> 00:00:31.140
They have very
particular types of needs

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:32.960
that come down to
matchmaking and dedicated

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:35.980
game-server scaling that we're
going to talk about today.

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:37.680
And those are the
type of games that we

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:39.060
want to talk about today.

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:41.080
There is a wide variety
of multiplayer games.

00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:42.570
But these are the
projects that we

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:44.278
want to talk about
today and the projects

00:00:44.278 --> 00:00:47.350
that we both work on as well.

00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:50.610
So some of you may be familiar
with this-- some of you

00:00:50.610 --> 00:00:52.140
maybe not.

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:54.300
This is-- generally
speaking-- watched

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:57.090
a traditional
architecture would look

00:00:57.090 --> 00:00:59.010
like for an online
multiplayer game.

00:00:59.010 --> 00:01:01.740
There are a variety of ways
in which one can do this--

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:02.820
just so I'm clear.

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:05.220
This is a very prevalent
way of doing it.

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:08.080
That could be a whole other
talk in and of itself.

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:10.350
But in this
particular example, we

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:12.780
might have a couple of players
that want to play our game.

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:15.570
They're like, cool, let's play
a game, this sounds awesome.

00:01:15.570 --> 00:01:18.510
And so they're going to connect
to some kind of matchmaker.

00:01:18.510 --> 00:01:21.270
Now, usually a matchmaker will
look at things like your skill

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:24.360
level, maybe your social graph--
who your friends are-- and put

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:26.610
you together in a group--
so that they can pair you

00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:26.970
together.

00:01:26.970 --> 00:01:29.345
So they think you're going to
have a really good gameplay

00:01:29.345 --> 00:01:30.330
experience.

00:01:30.330 --> 00:01:33.120
Once they've got you and
put you in this group,

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:37.120
they're going to go talk to some
kind of game server manager,

00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:37.620
right?

00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:38.340
We use this term--

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:39.965
Dedicated Game Server--
and some of you

00:01:39.965 --> 00:01:41.270
may not be familiar with it.

00:01:41.270 --> 00:01:44.010
Basically, it is
a full simulation

00:01:44.010 --> 00:01:46.435
of the game that happens
somewhere on the internet

00:01:46.435 --> 00:01:48.060
that your players
are, therefore, going

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:48.685
to connect you.

00:01:48.685 --> 00:01:50.977
This means that they can
share information about what's

00:01:50.977 --> 00:01:52.337
happening inside the game.

00:01:52.337 --> 00:01:54.670
The Dedicated Game Server can
tell all the players, hey,

00:01:54.670 --> 00:01:56.050
this is what's going on.

00:01:56.050 --> 00:01:57.930
And it also gives
us some control

00:01:57.930 --> 00:02:00.880
in that we can place these
game servers around the world

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:02.850
and what sort of latency
speed we can expect.

00:02:02.850 --> 00:02:05.640
Because we have a basic idea
of speed of light and network

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:07.600
speeds and good
things like that.

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:11.050
So this Game Server Manager's
job is basically to say,

00:02:11.050 --> 00:02:14.460
OK, I probably have a
huge number of machines.

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:17.220
They're probably distributed
around the world.

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:19.740
And I need to say, OK, let's
go find the machine that

00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:22.290
has some CPU resource
available and some memory,

00:02:22.290 --> 00:02:24.720
and start up a Game
Server Process on them,

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:26.820
and then get that going,
and then find the port,

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:29.212
and then send that all the
way back down the pipe--

00:02:29.212 --> 00:02:30.420
back down to the matchmakers.

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:32.970
So that those game servers
can get an IP and port--

00:02:32.970 --> 00:02:35.345
because they're going to
connect to an individual process

00:02:35.345 --> 00:02:36.100
on a machine.

00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:38.070
And then those game
players will both connect

00:02:38.070 --> 00:02:40.200
to the same IP and port
on one of those machines--

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:41.880
to that dedicated
Game Server instance--

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:43.620
so they can play
a game together.

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:46.240
So they're going to
share that simulation.

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:48.915
And this is how a lot of
games that we all play--

00:02:48.915 --> 00:02:50.790
if people play online
games, I'm guessing you

00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:52.440
do, because many of you here--

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:54.110
how a lot of them work.

00:02:54.110 --> 00:02:58.020
But a lot of this is a lot
of the time very bespoke.

00:02:58.020 --> 00:02:59.520
You kind of have
to do it yourself.

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:00.770
You have to build it yourself.

00:03:00.770 --> 00:03:02.280
And that's hard
and a lot of work.

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:03.210
And I don't know--

00:03:03.210 --> 00:03:04.585
I find it an
interesting problem,

00:03:04.585 --> 00:03:06.300
but I'm guessing a
lot of people don't.

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:08.402
So what we want to
talk about today two

00:03:08.402 --> 00:03:09.360
very particular things.

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:11.003
One, here, is matchmaking.

00:03:11.003 --> 00:03:12.420
Wouldn't it be
lovely if there was

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:14.760
some sort of, maybe,
open-source, standardized

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:18.307
matchmaking framework type thing
that helped solve this problem?

00:03:18.307 --> 00:03:20.640
And then also, here, when we
talk about this Game Server

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:23.780
Manager, coordinating game
servers over lots of machines.

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:25.530
Wouldn't it be nice
if there was some sort

00:03:25.530 --> 00:03:28.980
of open-source, standardized
Game Server Manager type thing?

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:30.420
Wouldn't that be lovely?

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:32.470
So let's talk about
both of those.

00:03:32.470 --> 00:03:35.220
But before we get into that,
we mentioned this a little bit

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:36.630
previously.

00:03:36.630 --> 00:03:38.280
Kubernetes is at
the core of both

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:40.620
of the solutions we're going
to be talking about today.

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:42.412
And I just want to
highlight this for three

00:03:42.412 --> 00:03:44.630
very important reasons.

00:03:44.630 --> 00:03:46.590
Both of these projects
run on top of Kubernetes

00:03:46.590 --> 00:03:47.800
in slightly different ways.

00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:49.258
And we'll talk
about that in a bit.

00:03:49.258 --> 00:03:50.617
But this is really important.

00:03:50.617 --> 00:03:51.700
There are several reasons.

00:03:51.700 --> 00:03:54.552
One of which is simplicity.

00:03:54.552 --> 00:03:56.760
So usually, when talking to
game companies that run--

00:03:56.760 --> 00:03:59.040
say-- services, such as
matchmaking, but also game

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:02.580
servers, they run very
different platforms for both.

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:05.100
Having a single platform
for both of these workloads

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:07.860
means that there is a
simplification of the knowledge

00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:10.380
you need your team, the
tools you need to use,

00:04:10.380 --> 00:04:11.600
and all that kind of stuff.

00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:13.920
It makes that much
easier for your teams

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:16.190
to basically either get up
and running and also do you

00:04:16.190 --> 00:04:18.260
day three day four
kind of operations.

00:04:18.260 --> 00:04:20.760
I think it goes without saying--
as many of you are probably

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:22.290
quite knowledgeable about--

00:04:22.290 --> 00:04:24.943
there's a huge ecosystem
of tooling here, right.

00:04:24.943 --> 00:04:26.610
Kubernetes was not
just built by Google,

00:04:26.610 --> 00:04:29.040
but a variety of partners
that did this work.

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:31.990
And there's all the tooling
that goes on around it.

00:04:31.990 --> 00:04:34.060
Finally-- and I think
most importantly,

00:04:34.060 --> 00:04:36.150
especially for
dedicated game servers--

00:04:36.150 --> 00:04:38.610
sometimes you just need
to put game servers

00:04:38.610 --> 00:04:42.270
in these kinds of
latency-sensitive applications

00:04:42.270 --> 00:04:46.020
in weird places around the world
just because sometimes players

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:49.327
just show up in odd places,
and you need to put them there.

00:04:49.327 --> 00:04:50.910
And maybe your cloud
covers it-- maybe

00:04:50.910 --> 00:04:52.830
we have great GK
coverage there-- awesome.

00:04:52.830 --> 00:04:54.480
But maybe sometimes we don't.

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:56.850
So having a standardized
platform like Kubernetes

00:04:56.850 --> 00:04:59.730
means that you can
take your entire stack

00:04:59.730 --> 00:05:01.630
and put it exactly
where your players need.

00:05:01.630 --> 00:05:03.600
And that's really
important to us.

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:06.390
So let's [? dig ?] in.

00:05:06.390 --> 00:05:08.460
JOSEPH HOLLEY: All
right, so we're

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:11.150
going to start talking a
little bit about Open Match.

00:05:11.150 --> 00:05:15.030
This is a framework that
we co-founded last year

00:05:15.030 --> 00:05:16.050
with Unity.

00:05:16.050 --> 00:05:19.380
It was announced at
Unite Berlin last summer.

00:05:19.380 --> 00:05:22.320
If you are interested
in learning more

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:25.290
about the founding of it and
our relationship with Unity,

00:05:25.290 --> 00:05:29.790
I recommend you go check out
the talk on YouTube about that.

00:05:29.790 --> 00:05:31.860
We are really focused--

00:05:31.860 --> 00:05:35.790
in this project-- on solving
a lot of the really basic

00:05:35.790 --> 00:05:37.560
plumbing problems
of matchmaking,

00:05:37.560 --> 00:05:40.710
like getting players connected
to the matchmaking service,

00:05:40.710 --> 00:05:43.200
integrating the matchmaking
service with your backend

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:46.630
infrastructure, but leaving
the bits in the middle--

00:05:46.630 --> 00:05:49.660
which is your custom
matchmaking logic--

00:05:49.660 --> 00:05:53.150
up to you, not really being
prescriptive about that at all.

00:05:53.150 --> 00:05:54.820
So it's very flexible.

00:05:54.820 --> 00:05:57.680
It's designed with APIs that
are horizontally scalable,

00:05:57.680 --> 00:05:59.710
so you can handle
your player load,

00:05:59.710 --> 00:06:01.900
even if you end up
with a really big hit.

00:06:01.900 --> 00:06:04.690
And it's designed-- as I
said-- to integrate well

00:06:04.690 --> 00:06:07.595
with your existing
processes or other tools

00:06:07.595 --> 00:06:08.470
that you want to use.

00:06:08.470 --> 00:06:12.293
It is written in Golang, but
your own, custom matchmaking

00:06:12.293 --> 00:06:13.960
logic, you can write
in any language you

00:06:13.960 --> 00:06:17.300
can put in a Docker container.

00:06:17.300 --> 00:06:18.670
So how it works--

00:06:18.670 --> 00:06:22.420
the game clients will
connect to the API

00:06:22.420 --> 00:06:26.200
that Open Match exposes
for the frontend.

00:06:26.200 --> 00:06:29.050
They pass in all of the
different attributes

00:06:29.050 --> 00:06:33.760
that you might want to
try and match players on.

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:36.705
Developers then write these
custom matchmaking functions--

00:06:36.705 --> 00:06:38.080
like I mentioned
before, that can

00:06:38.080 --> 00:06:39.400
be in any language you want.

00:06:39.400 --> 00:06:41.830
The only real
requirement is you have

00:06:41.830 --> 00:06:43.960
to be able to package it
in a Docker container,

00:06:43.960 --> 00:06:45.970
because this does
run on Kubernetes.

00:06:45.970 --> 00:06:48.970
And it needs to be
able to talk to Redis,

00:06:48.970 --> 00:06:53.710
which is the backend state
storage for Open Match.

00:06:53.710 --> 00:06:56.620
It allows you to run a lot of
these matchmaking functions

00:06:56.620 --> 00:07:01.060
concurrently, which is both
a way to handle scale and use

00:07:01.060 --> 00:07:04.390
the ability that Kubernetes
has to schedule automatically

00:07:04.390 --> 00:07:05.210
for you.

00:07:05.210 --> 00:07:07.750
But also to enable you
to try to iterate really

00:07:07.750 --> 00:07:11.347
rapidly on this logic piece
that is most important to you

00:07:11.347 --> 00:07:13.180
as a game developer--
of how do I figure out

00:07:13.180 --> 00:07:16.507
which players play together.

00:07:16.507 --> 00:07:19.090
So let's start with a demo, and
then I'll dive in a little bit

00:07:19.090 --> 00:07:22.660
further in a minute on
the actual architecture.

00:07:22.660 --> 00:07:24.580
Can we switch to the demo?

00:07:24.580 --> 00:07:25.550
That's great.

00:07:25.550 --> 00:07:26.650
Awesome.

00:07:26.650 --> 00:07:30.340
So what I have here is an
empty Kubernetes cluster.

00:07:30.340 --> 00:07:36.240
Let me quickly show you
that there is nothing in it.

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:38.790
Also important to note upfront--

00:07:38.790 --> 00:07:41.790
I am not very good at typing
and talking at the same time.

00:07:41.790 --> 00:07:45.820
I'll do my best, but
I'm not great at that.

00:07:45.820 --> 00:07:51.490
So right now, this project is
set up with a number of images.

00:07:51.490 --> 00:07:53.810
I've already pulled them--
set them up in the project.

00:07:53.810 --> 00:07:55.750
Most of these images
are available publicly

00:07:55.750 --> 00:07:58.120
from the Open Match repository.

00:07:58.120 --> 00:08:01.360
And we have a public
container registry

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:02.392
you can pull these from.

00:08:02.392 --> 00:08:03.100
But you can see--

00:08:03.100 --> 00:08:05.270
I've already got
the images there.

00:08:05.270 --> 00:08:08.970
So the first thing I'm going to
do is just install Open Match,

00:08:08.970 --> 00:08:11.090
and let you see what the
process of that is like.

00:08:11.090 --> 00:08:12.730
So you can see how simple it is.

00:08:12.730 --> 00:08:15.760
The first thing I'm going to do
is create a Kubernetes config

00:08:15.760 --> 00:08:16.540
map.

00:08:16.540 --> 00:08:19.930
This contains all the
configuration for Open Match.

00:08:19.930 --> 00:08:23.200
If anyone here has actually gone
and looked at the Open Match

00:08:23.200 --> 00:08:26.320
repository and seen what the
configuration looks like,

00:08:26.320 --> 00:08:28.270
I am applying-- almost exactly--

00:08:28.270 --> 00:08:31.427
the one that's in the
example on the repository.

00:08:31.427 --> 00:08:33.010
The only difference--
you can probably

00:08:33.010 --> 00:08:35.635
guess from the title there-- is
I turned off the debug logging.

00:08:35.635 --> 00:08:38.470
Because that does not
make for a good demo.

00:08:38.470 --> 00:08:41.690
So first of all, we're going to
stand up an instance of Redis.

00:08:41.690 --> 00:08:46.621
This is just running
Redis within Kubernetes.

00:08:46.621 --> 00:08:51.430
It is utilizing a Kubernetes
secret for its username

00:08:51.430 --> 00:08:54.940
and password to log into Redis.

00:08:54.940 --> 00:08:56.560
Now I'm going to
install Open Match.

00:08:56.560 --> 00:09:00.610
This is a series of Kubernetes
deployments and services

00:09:00.610 --> 00:09:01.970
in front of them.

00:09:01.970 --> 00:09:03.940
It makes a few--

00:09:03.940 --> 00:09:05.305
let me show you those.

00:09:08.320 --> 00:09:13.050
So you can see, here, we
have six pods running.

00:09:13.050 --> 00:09:15.660
So Redis and then five
services from Open Match--

00:09:15.660 --> 00:09:17.400
that's the om at the
beginning of those.

00:09:20.530 --> 00:09:23.440
These are run as deployments,
so if something were to happen,

00:09:23.440 --> 00:09:26.840
Kubernetes will take care of
starting it back up for you.

00:09:26.840 --> 00:09:29.980
It also allows you to very
easily scale those deployments

00:09:29.980 --> 00:09:35.210
if you get a big hit and you
need to handle more players.

00:09:35.210 --> 00:09:38.690
So the first thing I'm going to
do after actually standing up

00:09:38.690 --> 00:09:41.325
Open Match is stick some
players in Open Match.

00:09:41.325 --> 00:09:43.450
This part's going to take
just a second, because it

00:09:43.450 --> 00:09:44.575
has to pull down the image.

00:09:44.575 --> 00:09:47.570
I just made a brand new
cluster for this demo.

00:09:47.570 --> 00:09:52.960
So what this particular pod
is going to do is spin up.

00:09:52.960 --> 00:09:56.212
This pod-- just like
