00:09:57.670 --> 00:10:02.017
available for public on the
Open Match public registry.

00:10:02.017 --> 00:10:03.100
But it's going to spin up.

00:10:03.100 --> 00:10:07.600
It's going to queue
10,000 players

00:10:07.600 --> 00:10:09.560
in Open Match seven times.

00:10:09.560 --> 00:10:13.370
That's the seven cycles with
10,000 players per cycle.

00:10:13.370 --> 00:10:15.120
This is just to get a
bunch of players in,

00:10:15.120 --> 00:10:16.537
so I can actually
show you what it

00:10:16.537 --> 00:10:21.185
looks like whenever Open Match
is doing your matching logic.

00:10:21.185 --> 00:10:22.060
So I'll let that run.

00:10:22.060 --> 00:10:23.830
It'll take just a second.

00:10:23.830 --> 00:10:25.420
And then I have
a matchmaker that

00:10:25.420 --> 00:10:27.370
is written against Open Match.

00:10:27.370 --> 00:10:31.150
And I use that kind of
language intentionally.

00:10:31.150 --> 00:10:33.520
Open Match is not in
itself a matchmaker.

00:10:33.520 --> 00:10:36.700
It is a framework for
writing scalable matchmakers

00:10:36.700 --> 00:10:38.620
on top of Kubernetes.

00:10:38.620 --> 00:10:42.070
So this is the example
matchmaker, which

00:10:42.070 --> 00:10:43.780
is currently on the repository.

00:10:43.780 --> 00:10:45.290
It's not very big.

00:10:45.290 --> 00:10:53.560
I think it's probably
220 or 250 lines of code.

00:10:53.560 --> 00:10:57.940
Therefore, it is really basic,
but what it does allow me to do

00:10:57.940 --> 00:11:01.850
is run a lot of concurrent
matchmaking functions.

00:11:01.850 --> 00:11:04.992
So you can kind of see how
scaling works in Open Match.

00:11:04.992 --> 00:11:06.700
So in general, the
approach of Open Match

00:11:06.700 --> 00:11:09.400
is you have this very large
player population-- mine,

00:11:09.400 --> 00:11:11.770
right now, is 70,000 people.

00:11:11.770 --> 00:11:15.010
I'm going to try and
split up that population

00:11:15.010 --> 00:11:16.540
into a few different
chunks and then

00:11:16.540 --> 00:11:19.720
look within all of those chunks,
simultaneously, for matches.

00:11:19.720 --> 00:11:21.730
So it'll allow me to
get a lot of matches

00:11:21.730 --> 00:11:25.090
at once, without having to
chew through all of the players

00:11:25.090 --> 00:11:27.160
in giant piles of
sequential code.

00:11:30.590 --> 00:11:34.740
One of my deployments sometimes
takes a minute to start.

00:11:34.740 --> 00:11:36.350
So the first loop--

00:11:36.350 --> 00:11:38.990
I didn't give it long enough
to start off with the demo.

00:11:38.990 --> 00:11:40.400
But you can see here.

00:11:40.400 --> 00:11:43.370
I'm looking for two teams
of eight, four times,

00:11:43.370 --> 00:11:45.440
in four different parts
of the player population.

00:11:45.440 --> 00:11:47.390
You can see the pools here.

00:11:47.390 --> 00:11:53.150
There between 10,000 and 15,000
players, roughly, per pool.

00:11:53.150 --> 00:11:55.658
Within those pools, I'm
only looking for 16 players.

00:11:55.658 --> 00:11:57.200
So this didn't take
very long at all.

00:11:57.200 --> 00:12:01.400
You can see I ran all four
of those concurrently in just

00:12:01.400 --> 00:12:03.240
a little over two seconds.

00:12:03.240 --> 00:12:05.390
So that's not very
interesting in terms of scale.

00:12:05.390 --> 00:12:06.765
Open Match is
definitely designed

00:12:06.765 --> 00:12:08.550
to go a lot wider than that.

00:12:08.550 --> 00:12:10.460
So this time, I'm
going to split it up

00:12:10.460 --> 00:12:14.103
into 400 different
buckets that I'm

00:12:14.103 --> 00:12:15.520
looking for players
in, and you're

00:12:15.520 --> 00:12:18.410
going to see a lot of these go
by with, insufficient players.

00:12:18.410 --> 00:12:20.612
That means the part of
the player population I'm

00:12:20.612 --> 00:12:22.070
looking at-- there
literally aren't

00:12:22.070 --> 00:12:24.380
even 16 players in that
part of the population

00:12:24.380 --> 00:12:26.270
to make a match out of.

00:12:26.270 --> 00:12:28.550
So what this is
demonstrating is running

00:12:28.550 --> 00:12:31.670
400 copies of your match
logic concurrently.

00:12:31.670 --> 00:12:34.230
This usually takes around 20
or 30 seconds to complete.

00:12:34.230 --> 00:12:35.760
Yeah, 24 seconds.

00:12:35.760 --> 00:12:37.100
So we ran 400 copies.

00:12:37.100 --> 00:12:38.713
About 2/3 of those
found a match.

00:12:38.713 --> 00:12:40.130
The other third
did not, because I

00:12:40.130 --> 00:12:41.960
was looking at parts of the
population where there just

00:12:41.960 --> 00:12:43.430
weren't enough players there.

00:12:43.430 --> 00:12:45.770
You can see this got us a
lot more throughput though.

00:12:45.770 --> 00:12:50.790
This matched 4,288
players and managed

00:12:50.790 --> 00:12:55.850
to match about 180 players per
second that it was searching.

00:12:55.850 --> 00:13:00.560
So that's kind of how you
achieve scale with Open Match.

00:13:00.560 --> 00:13:02.660
So can we go back to the slides?

00:13:02.660 --> 00:13:06.690
I'm going to briefly go
over the architecture.

00:13:06.690 --> 00:13:11.750
And then Mark is going to
talk about using Agones

00:13:11.750 --> 00:13:17.518
So this architecture is
basically the same diagram

00:13:17.518 --> 00:13:19.060
that Mark showed
earlier-- only we've

00:13:19.060 --> 00:13:20.770
taken that box that
just said matchmaker

00:13:20.770 --> 00:13:24.550
and blown it up to show you
all the parts of Open Match.

00:13:24.550 --> 00:13:26.620
So to begin with,
the Game Client

00:13:26.620 --> 00:13:29.450
would talk to the
Open Match frontend.

00:13:29.450 --> 00:13:33.730
The frontend writes the
player attributes to Redis.

00:13:33.730 --> 00:13:38.380
Your Server Manager,
which can be anything--

00:13:38.380 --> 00:13:40.390
maybe you have bespoke
code, maybe you're using

00:13:40.390 --> 00:13:43.200
an off-the-shelf solution,
maybe you're using Agones--

00:13:43.200 --> 00:13:44.680
I would recommend it--

00:13:44.680 --> 00:13:48.250
would talk to the backend
API of Open Match.

00:13:48.250 --> 00:13:51.250
That kicks off your
custom match logic.

00:13:51.250 --> 00:13:54.460
Your custom match logic gets to
read and write data from Redis.

00:13:54.460 --> 00:13:56.940
It can read and write
it directly if you need

00:13:56.940 --> 00:13:58.570
that level of customizability.

00:13:58.570 --> 00:14:00.460
We also offer an API
in front of Redis

00:14:00.460 --> 00:14:04.420
to do really common matchmaking
things like filtering players

00:14:04.420 --> 00:14:08.410
or retrieving large pools of
players from Redis at once.

00:14:08.410 --> 00:14:10.870
The Server Manager will
then figure out which

00:14:10.870 --> 00:14:13.450
players go on which servers.

00:14:13.450 --> 00:14:15.430
So it'll probably
pick a server and get

00:14:15.430 --> 00:14:17.830
the IP and port of that server.

00:14:17.830 --> 00:14:20.770
That gets actually passed
back out, through Open Match,

00:14:20.770 --> 00:14:25.180
to your game client app,
which at this point,

00:14:25.180 --> 00:14:28.180
goes and connects directly
to the dedicated game server.

00:14:28.180 --> 00:14:32.150
That's all really similar to
what Mark showed you earlier.

00:14:32.150 --> 00:14:34.250
So this is already
an improvement.

00:14:34.250 --> 00:14:38.350
But we still have some kind
of bespoke server management

00:14:38.350 --> 00:14:39.460
system.

00:14:39.460 --> 00:14:42.040
So Mark is going to talk
to us about replacing that

00:14:42.040 --> 00:14:43.330
with Agones.

00:14:43.330 --> 00:14:44.902
Mark?

00:14:44.902 --> 00:14:47.550
MARK MANDEL: Awesome, so
yeah, like Joseph said,

00:14:47.550 --> 00:14:51.630
we have this other part that
we also need to replace.

00:14:51.630 --> 00:14:55.380
So strangely enough-- OK, so
we have another open source

00:14:55.380 --> 00:14:58.920
project, called Agones,
for orchestrating, scaling,

00:14:58.920 --> 00:15:01.803
and hosting your
dedicated game servers.

00:15:01.803 --> 00:15:02.970
This has been a fun project.

00:15:02.970 --> 00:15:05.012
We've been working on it
for actually over a year

00:15:05.012 --> 00:15:06.350
now-- now that I think about it.

00:15:06.350 --> 00:15:08.970
And it is built as an
extension of Kubernetes

00:15:08.970 --> 00:15:10.600
that we'll see in a minute.

00:15:10.600 --> 00:15:14.220
So it's basically a thing that
puts itself inside Kubernetes.

00:15:14.220 --> 00:15:16.860
So suddenly Kubernetes
is like, awesome,

00:15:16.860 --> 00:15:18.365
I know how game servers work.

00:15:18.365 --> 00:15:19.740
This is actually
really important

00:15:19.740 --> 00:15:22.320
if people aren't that familiar
with dedicated game servers.

00:15:22.320 --> 00:15:24.112
They have this interesting
thing where they

00:15:24.112 --> 00:15:26.730
go from stateless to stateful.

00:15:26.730 --> 00:15:29.280
So when you don't have any
players on a game server,

00:15:29.280 --> 00:15:31.765
no one seems to mind
if you kill them.

00:15:31.765 --> 00:15:33.390
When you have players
on a game server,

00:15:33.390 --> 00:15:35.280
people get really
upset if you just

00:15:35.280 --> 00:15:36.855
ruin their gameplay experience.

00:15:36.855 --> 00:15:37.980
They post things on Reddit.

00:15:37.980 --> 00:15:38.490
It's awful.

00:15:38.490 --> 00:15:40.080
No one likes that.

00:15:40.080 --> 00:15:43.320
So Agones's real capability--
and part of its core strength--

00:15:43.320 --> 00:15:47.740
is really managing that
transition between the two.

00:15:47.740 --> 00:15:50.550
Now, what's really awesome
is we didn't do this alone.

00:15:50.550 --> 00:15:53.520
Much like Joseph was talking
about with Open Match--

00:15:53.520 --> 00:15:55.620
they built that in
collaboration with Unity.

00:15:55.620 --> 00:15:57.870
We've been working on this
quite closely with Ubisoft

00:15:57.870 --> 00:15:59.310
since the beginning.

00:15:59.310 --> 00:16:02.590
It's been a really great
collaboration between us.

00:16:02.590 --> 00:16:05.610
They've been able to bring
sort of their experience

00:16:05.610 --> 00:16:08.370
and their knowledge around
how to run large game server

00:16:08.370 --> 00:16:09.087
workloads.

00:16:09.087 --> 00:16:10.920
And we have been able
to bring our knowledge

00:16:10.920 --> 00:16:14.037
to work on Kubernetes and
open source communities.

00:16:14.037 --> 00:16:15.120
So it's been really great.

00:16:15.120 --> 00:16:16.578
And we've also
gotten contributions

00:16:16.578 --> 00:16:19.720
from a wide variety of other
people since then as well.

00:16:19.720 --> 00:16:21.330
So I'm going to
actually show you

00:16:21.330 --> 00:16:22.910
how to do some stuff on Agones.

00:16:22.910 --> 00:16:24.660
And here I'll actually
show you some code.

00:16:24.660 --> 00:16:26.560
And you can actually
see how it happens.

00:16:26.560 --> 00:16:28.890
So potentially
unsurprisingly, If you

00:16:28.890 --> 00:16:31.650
want to start having a game
server and run it on Kubernetes

00:16:31.650 --> 00:16:34.590
and run it on Agones, it
goes inside a container.

00:16:34.590 --> 00:16:36.297
Nothing more special than that.

00:16:36.297 --> 00:16:38.880
I'm sure that's something a lot
of you are very familiar with.

00:16:38.880 --> 00:16:42.270
The only "special thing," quote
unquote, that we have is we

00:16:42.270 --> 00:16:46.980
do have an integrated client SDK
that goes into the game server.

00:16:46.980 --> 00:16:50.608
We have options available
in C++, Go, REST--

00:16:50.608 --> 00:16:52.150
we actually have a
Rust one as well--

00:16:52.150 --> 00:16:53.430
It's getting updated soon.

00:16:53.430 --> 00:16:56.335
This is all either
GRPC-based or REST-based.

00:16:56.335 --> 00:16:57.960
So the real short
answer here is if you

00:16:57.960 --> 00:16:59.880
have a language
that need support,

00:16:59.880 --> 00:17:00.842
we have an SDK for you.

00:17:00.842 --> 00:17:02.800
I actually realized we
also have a Node.js one.

00:17:02.800 --> 00:17:04.380
And it's not on my slide,
because it just came

00:17:04.380 --> 00:17:05.640
in the most recent release.

00:17:05.640 --> 00:17:07.440
And I haven't updated
the slide yet.

00:17:07.440 --> 00:17:10.369
But Yeah if you want to
integrate it, we can do it.

00:17:10.369 --> 00:17:13.470
And this SDK is really just
about managing game server

00:17:13.470 --> 00:17:15.763
health lifecycle, which is
just a little bit different,

00:17:15.763 --> 00:17:18.180
knowing whether the game server
is ready to accept players

00:17:18.180 --> 00:17:20.579
or not, looking at
the configuration--

00:17:20.579 --> 00:17:21.310
things like that.

00:17:21.310 --> 00:17:25.220
Basically standard things
you need for game servers.

00:17:25.220 --> 00:17:31.250
So I said before that Agones
is an extension of Kubernetes.

00:17:31.250 --> 00:17:33.875
So when you've used Kubernetes--
and I think a lot of you have,

00:17:33.875 --> 00:17:34.375
right?

00:17:34.375 --> 00:17:36.700
You're very familiar with
services-- with deployments,

00:17:36.700 --> 00:17:37.200
right?

00:17:37.200 --> 00:17:39.650
You have these nouns that
exist inside Kubernetes.

00:17:39.650 --> 00:17:42.530
Well, once you install
Agones, now this new noun,

00:17:42.530 --> 00:17:44.850
called GameServer, is a reality.

00:17:44.850 --> 00:17:46.850
So we can say to
Kubernetes, hey, Kubernetes,

00:17:46.850 --> 00:17:48.680
I would really
like a game server.

00:17:48.680 --> 00:17:50.570
I would really like to
name this game server

00:17:50.570 --> 00:17:52.250
xonotic, which is the
example we're actually

00:17:52.250 --> 00:17:53.042
going to use today.

00:17:53.042 --> 00:17:55.990
It's a free-to-play,
open-source FPS.

00:17:55.990 --> 00:17:58.520
And then we can specify things
about this game server spec

00:17:58.520 --> 00:18:00.660
that we want to have
available to us.

00:18:00.660 --> 00:18:03.050
So one of the things
that Agones also does

00:18:03.050 --> 00:18:05.030
is set up that direct
port connection.

00:18:05.030 --> 00:18:07.910
So it'll do that dynamic
port allocation for you

00:18:07.910 --> 00:18:09.890
to set up the port that
that information is

00:18:09.890 --> 00:18:12.150
routed to inside the
game server container.

00:18:12.150 --> 00:18:13.820
So here, we specify
that the game

00:18:13.820 --> 00:18:17.180
server is going to start up
port 2,600, but it's dynamic.

00:18:17.180 --> 00:18:19.517
So it's going to give us
a port once we're done.

00:18:19.517 --> 00:18:21.350
And finally, we're going
to say, hey, what's

00:18:21.350 --> 00:18:23.030
my game server container?

00:18:23.030 --> 00:18:24.560
What have I actually
put in here?

00:18:24.560 --> 00:18:27.060
Now for those of you who are
more familiar with Kubernetes--

00:18:27.060 --> 00:18:29.370
which many of you are-- you
have a full pod spec here.

00:18:29.370 --> 00:18:31.037
So if you want to do
config maps, volume

00:18:31.037 --> 00:18:35.420
mounts, multiple containers, et
cetera, et cetera, you're fine.

00:18:35.420 --> 00:18:38.150
Now, this is great
for a single server.

00:18:38.150 --> 00:18:40.145
In production-- in
reality, when you really

00:18:40.145 --> 00:18:42.020
want to do this-- usually
what you want to do

00:18:42.020 --> 00:18:45.800
is actually have a large
group of pre-spun game servers

00:18:45.800 --> 00:18:47.930
that are sitting there
idle, waiting for players.

00:18:47.930 --> 00:18:50.540
Sometimes game servers can
take a little while to spin up.

00:18:50.540 --> 00:18:54.650
So much as we have pods and
deployments inside Kubernetes,

00:18:54.650 --> 00:18:56.880
we have GameServers and Fleets.

00:18:56.880 --> 00:18:59.370
So Fleets-- again, like we
were talking about previously--

00:18:59.370 --> 00:19:02.420
are very aware of that change
in lifecycle between stateless

00:19:02.420 --> 00:19:03.770
and stateful.

00:19:03.770 --> 00:19:06.090
We'll go a little bit
into that in a minute.

00:19:06.090 --> 00:19:08.490
But here, we have a Fleet.

00:19:08.490 --> 00:19:11.150
It looks very much like a
deployment in that we specify

00:19:11.150 --> 00:19:12.320
how many replicas we want.

00:19:12.320 --> 00:19:13.050
It can be two.

00:19:13.050 --> 00:19:15.152
It can be 2,000-- up to you.

00:19:15.152 --> 00:19:17.360
And then, we have a template,
which is our GameServer

00:19:17.360 --> 00:19:18.110
configuration.

00:19:18.110 --> 00:19:21.380
And that's going to spin up
a large chunk of GameServers

00:19:21.380 --> 00:19:23.460
for us, which is awesome.

00:19:23.460 --> 00:19:26.090
Now the interesting question
you might have at this stage--

00:19:26.090 --> 00:19:28.040
but Mark, you make
these direct connections

00:19:28.040 --> 00:19:30.560
to these GameServers
by IP and port.

00:19:30.560 --> 00:19:31.952
I don't have a load balancer.

00:19:31.952 --> 00:19:33.410
I don't have any
of the usual tools

00:19:33.410 --> 00:19:34.820
that I may be familiar with.

00:19:34.820 --> 00:19:38.010
How do I get one of these
out in an atomic manner.

00:19:38.010 --> 00:19:42.230
So we have this concept inside
Agones called an Allocation.

00:19:42.230 --> 00:19:44.210
Basically the job
of an Allocation

00:19:44.210 --> 00:19:47.420
is to say, hey, out of this
big group of game servers

00:19:47.420 --> 00:19:50.090
can I please have one
atomically and set it to what

00:19:50.090 --> 00:19:51.530
we call an allocated state?

00:19:51.530 --> 00:19:53.390
So we know that it
has players on it.

00:19:53.390 --> 00:19:54.620
And just do that work for me.

00:19:54.620 --> 00:19:55.580
Do it in the right way.

00:19:55.580 --> 00:19:58.527
Find the right place inside the
cluster, et cetera, et cetera.

00:19:58.527 --> 00:20:00.860
So what we can do is we can
send another custom resource

00:20:00.860 --> 00:20:01.970
definition.

00:20:01.970 --> 00:20:03.900
Another thing called a
GameServerAllocation--

00:20:03.900 --> 00:20:05.780
and we can say--

00:20:05.780 --> 00:20:08.600
in this particular instance--
hey, for this particular fleet,

00:20:08.600 --> 00:20:11.450
called xonotic, give me
one out of this pool,

00:20:11.450 --> 00:20:13.032
hand it back to you atomically.

00:20:13.032 --> 00:20:14.990
Just give it to me, and
send it to allocate it.

00:20:14.990 --> 00:20:16.717
And give me its details, please.

00:20:16.717 --> 00:20:19.050
We can do actually a lot more
with GameServerAllocation.

00:20:19.050 --> 00:20:21.258
We've got some pretty complex
selector logic in there

00:20:21.258 --> 00:20:21.950
if you need it.

00:20:21.950 --> 00:20:24.640
But this is a simple example.

00:20:24.640 --> 00:20:27.790
So once we've done that, we
can then play a game on it.

00:20:27.790 --> 00:20:30.930
So why don't I show
you that it's action?

00:20:30.930 --> 00:20:33.185
Can I have-- switch
over to demo, please.

00:20:36.100 --> 00:20:36.790
Do, do, do.

00:20:39.410 --> 00:20:40.250
Thank you very much.

00:20:43.870 --> 00:20:46.160
Is that readable in the back?

00:20:46.160 --> 00:20:46.660
Thumbs up?

00:20:46.660 --> 00:20:47.260
Perfect.

00:20:47.260 --> 00:20:47.960
Awesome.

00:20:47.960 --> 00:20:49.570
So the first thing I'm going
to do-- much like Joseph--

00:20:49.570 --> 00:20:50.820
we're going to install Agones.

00:20:50.820 --> 00:20:53.500
I'm going to use Helm--
it's a package manager--

00:20:53.500 --> 00:20:54.340
to install Agones.

00:20:54.340 --> 00:20:58.350
You can use straight
YAML if you want.

00:20:58.350 --> 00:21:03.770
--name=agones
--namespace=agones-system

00:21:03.770 --> 00:21:04.960
agones/agones.

00:21:04.960 --> 00:21:06.460
So if you're not
familiar with Helm,

00:21:06.460 --> 00:21:09.940
Helm is a package manager for
Kubernetes to basically install

00:21:09.940 --> 00:21:11.520
a bunch of stuff.

00:21:11.520 --> 00:21:14.170
So that's kind of as complicated
as it is to get it up

00:21:14.170 --> 00:21:15.280
and running.

00:21:15.280 --> 00:21:17.770
We want to see what
we have in there.

00:21:17.770 --> 00:21:19.457
agones-system, right?

00:21:19.457 --> 00:21:21.040
So we have a bunch
of things in there.

00:21:21.040 --> 00:21:22.790
There's a controller--
all sorts of stuff.

00:21:22.790 --> 00:21:25.280
But now we have
Agones installed.

00:21:25.280 --> 00:21:27.453
Now Kubernetes understands
what GameServers are.

00:21:27.453 --> 00:21:29.620
And this actually runs
throughout the entire system,

00:21:29.620 --> 00:21:31.703
which I actually think is
really, really exciting.

00:21:31.703 --> 00:21:34.332
So I can actually say,
kubectl get gameservers.

00:21:34.332 --> 00:21:36.040
And this is something
it now understands.

00:21:36.040 --> 00:21:37.707
It's going to say we
don't have any yet,

00:21:37.707 --> 00:21:39.730
but that's actually
really, really cool.

00:21:39.730 --> 00:21:42.040
So let's create a
fleet of game servers.

00:21:42.040 --> 00:21:44.450
We saw that xonotic
one we had previously.

00:21:44.450 --> 00:21:48.600
So I'll just go apply -f
./examples/xonotic/fleet.yaml.

00:21:48.600 --> 00:21:50.100
Now, everything I'm
want to do here,

00:21:50.100 --> 00:21:51.970
I'm just going to do
through YAML files.

00:21:51.970 --> 00:21:53.710
You may be very
familiar with this.

00:21:53.710 --> 00:21:56.620
You could do this through
the Kubernetes API as well.

00:21:56.620 --> 00:22:00.340
You can change one for
the other as needed.

00:22:00.340 --> 00:22:02.170
So now I can say,
hey, get gameservers.

00:22:02.170 --> 00:22:03.670
Let's see if those
are up and ready.

00:22:03.670 --> 00:22:04.480
And they are.

00:22:04.480 --> 00:22:05.680
So we can see-- here's
some information.

00:22:05.680 --> 00:22:07.120
We have a couple of
GameServers there.

00:22:07.120 --> 00:22:08.495
If you remember
from the example,

00:22:08.495 --> 00:22:09.610
we asked for having two.

00:22:09.610 --> 00:22:10.990
They're both in the state ready.

00:22:10.990 --> 00:22:12.740
So they're actually
ready to be allocated.

00:22:12.740 --> 00:22:14.110
We can do stuff with them.

00:22:14.110 --> 00:22:15.260
And that's all super nice.

00:22:15.260 --> 00:22:18.810
We can actually look at this
[? as ?] from Fleets as well.

00:22:18.810 --> 00:22:21.166
We can see, here-- we
have two that are ready.

00:22:21.166 --> 00:22:23.270
But two game servers
is boring, right?

00:22:23.270 --> 00:22:24.150
That's no fun.

00:22:24.150 --> 00:22:25.775
I mean, we're going
to get really huge,

00:22:25.775 --> 00:22:29.630
but we can scale fleet
--replicatas=200.

00:22:29.630 --> 00:22:31.422
Let's just say 200.

00:22:31.422 --> 00:22:34.520
Oh, I need tell it what fleet.

00:22:34.520 --> 00:22:35.960
There we go.

00:22:35.960 --> 00:22:38.080
So now we can put
that up to 200.

00:22:38.080 --> 00:22:43.770
If we want to look at that
here, we can get those details.

00:22:43.770 --> 00:22:45.950
That's going to take a
little while to come up.

00:22:45.950 --> 00:22:47.492
Come on, you can do
better than that.

00:22:47.492 --> 00:22:48.380
There we go, 74.

00:22:48.380 --> 00:22:51.200
But we actually look at
this in a different way too.

00:22:51.200 --> 00:22:52.980
I'm going to actually
refresh this.

00:22:52.980 --> 00:22:57.860
So we have inbuilt Grafana
dashboards and metrics

00:22:57.860 --> 00:22:58.700
that are available.

00:22:58.700 --> 00:22:59.950
Has that gone down in between?

00:23:02.510 --> 00:23:04.940
Is that going to run?

00:23:04.940 --> 00:23:07.563
Yeah, let's just rerun that?

00:23:07.563 --> 00:23:08.860
There we go.

00:23:08.860 --> 00:23:09.910
There we go, sweet.

00:23:09.910 --> 00:23:11.290
So we have inbuilt metrics--

00:23:11.290 --> 00:23:12.203
Grafana dashboards.

00:23:12.203 --> 00:23:13.620
We can also export
to Stackdriver.

00:23:13.620 --> 00:23:17.160
Basically everything comes out
through and through OpenCensus,

00:23:17.160 --> 00:23:19.290
so you can send it
just about anywhere

00:23:19.290 --> 00:23:21.750
to do all the stuff that you
need to do, as well, to manage

00:23:21.750 --> 00:23:23.260
your game servers.

00:23:23.260 --> 00:23:26.350
So here, we can see how
many are starting up,

00:23:26.350 --> 00:23:29.190
how many are allocated,
how many are ready.

00:23:29.190 --> 00:23:31.620
This is running a
little slow behind.

00:23:31.620 --> 00:23:34.340
Usually it pings about
every 30 seconds.

00:23:34.340 --> 00:23:34.840
There we go.

00:23:34.840 --> 00:23:35.610
That's better.

00:23:35.610 --> 00:23:36.540
So we've got 100--
you can't see.

00:23:36.540 --> 00:23:38.623
That that's tiny, but you
can take my word for it.

00:23:38.623 --> 00:23:40.080
It's about 186.

00:23:40.080 --> 00:23:40.760
There we go.

00:23:40.760 --> 00:23:41.490
We can see rates.

00:23:41.490 --> 00:23:43.470
And there's a variety
of metrics that we can

00:23:43.470 --> 00:23:47.670
take advantage of during this

00:23:47.670 --> 00:23:50.350
We'll go back to here.

00:23:50.350 --> 00:23:54.890
So if we look at our fleet--
all right, so we have 200 ready.

00:23:54.890 --> 00:23:57.350
They came up pretty quick,
which is super nice.

00:23:57.350 --> 00:24:01.830
Let's allocate one
out of this set.

00:24:01.830 --> 00:24:02.987
create -f

00:24:02.987 --> 00:24:05.070
Usually this would be
something you would probably

00:24:05.070 --> 00:24:06.090
do through the API.

00:24:06.090 --> 00:24:08.730
Your matchmaker would call this
and say, hey, can I allocate?

00:24:08.730 --> 00:24:10.188
We might show that
a little later--

00:24:12.370 --> 00:24:14.710
allocation-- I'm
going to go -o yaml,

00:24:14.710 --> 00:24:18.480
so it'll return to me exactly
the results of what it's done.

00:24:18.480 --> 00:24:19.638
So we'll do that.

00:24:19.638 --> 00:24:21.430
I'm not going to worry
about what's up top.

00:24:21.430 --> 00:24:22.722
I'm just going to look at here.

00:24:22.722 --> 00:24:26.280
So here, we can see-- we've
got a particular GameServer--

00:24:26.280 --> 00:24:27.370
awesome.

00:24:27.370 --> 00:24:29.100
Its state has been
set to allocated.

00:24:29.100 --> 00:24:31.610
And here are its details.

00:24:31.610 --> 00:24:33.650
So we have an IP and a port.

00:24:33.650 --> 00:24:36.150
So we'll do the fun thing that
everyone really wants to see.

00:24:36.150 --> 00:24:37.590
Let's actually
play a game on here

00:24:37.590 --> 00:24:40.580
and see if it works
over this network.

00:24:40.580 --> 00:24:41.315
So--

00:24:41.315 --> 00:24:42.790
JOSEPH HOLLEY:
Live demo fun time.

00:24:42.790 --> 00:24:43.915
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, exactly.

00:24:43.915 --> 00:24:44.943
So let's run that.

00:24:44.943 --> 00:24:46.610
I'm just going to use
that over a touch,

00:24:46.610 --> 00:24:48.725
so that I can see this.

00:24:48.725 --> 00:24:50.760
And let's see if I can type it.

00:24:50.760 --> 00:24:53.150
Yeah, perfect.

00:24:53.150 --> 00:24:53.650
Excellent.

00:24:53.650 --> 00:24:54.860
So this is xonotic.

00:24:54.860 --> 00:24:58.030
This is the open-source
first person shooter

00:24:58.030 --> 00:24:59.905
that we were talking
about previously.

00:24:59.905 --> 00:25:02.177
.230: --where are we?

00:25:02.177 --> 00:25:03.760
What's the
dynamically-allocated port?

00:25:03.760 --> 00:25:05.635
7162.

00:25:05.635 --> 00:25:06.640
I'll Click Join.

00:25:06.640 --> 00:25:08.210
The black screen
is actually good.

00:25:08.210 --> 00:25:08.980
I don't know why.

00:25:08.980 --> 00:25:11.022
They just made the game
to give me heart attacks.

00:25:13.580 --> 00:25:16.362
That's just how
they like to roll.

00:25:16.362 --> 00:25:17.820
Come on, you can
do it-- beautiful.

00:25:17.820 --> 00:25:20.310
OK, so now I'm going to play
this on my trackpad, which

00:25:20.310 --> 00:25:21.840
will be hilarious.

00:25:21.840 --> 00:25:24.090
Let's see if I can find some
of the other players that

00:25:24.090 --> 00:25:24.810
are about.

00:25:24.810 --> 00:25:26.830
Come on.

00:25:26.830 --> 00:25:29.295
OK, this always happens--
whenever I'm playing alone,

00:25:29.295 --> 00:25:30.420
there's players everywhere.

00:25:30.420 --> 00:25:32.603
And then as soon as I get
in front of an audience,

00:25:32.603 --> 00:25:33.270
nobody's around.

00:25:33.270 --> 00:25:33.770
There we go.

00:25:33.770 --> 00:25:34.530
There's some bots.

00:25:34.530 --> 00:25:37.643
So there's some rockets-- yay.

00:25:37.643 --> 00:25:39.810
So this is me playing Agones
on a Kubernetes cluster

00:25:39.810 --> 00:25:41.010
that's actually
running US West--

00:25:41.010 --> 00:25:41.910
JOSEPH HOLLEY: East?

00:25:41.910 --> 00:25:43.200
MARK MANDEL: No, West--

00:25:43.200 --> 00:25:45.153
whichever one is closest to us.

00:25:45.153 --> 00:25:46.320
And it's all working, right?

00:25:46.320 --> 00:25:47.695
You can see-- I'm
playing a game.

00:25:47.695 --> 00:25:49.110
And it's pretty awesome.

00:25:49.110 --> 00:25:51.720
So that's cool, but I want
to show off a few more things

00:25:51.720 --> 00:25:53.200
before we finish up this demo.

00:25:53.200 --> 00:25:54.533
So I'm just going to close that.

00:25:57.190 --> 00:25:59.710
We can see, here, we have one
game server that's allocated,

00:25:59.710 --> 00:25:59.860
right?

00:25:59.860 --> 00:26:01.420
There's a player playing on it.

00:26:01.420 --> 00:26:03.192
Let's allocate a couple more.

00:26:03.192 --> 00:26:04.440
I just want a few more.

00:26:06.832 --> 00:26:08.790
And maybe at this point
in time, we're actually

00:26:08.790 --> 00:26:09.660
like oh, you know what?

00:26:09.660 --> 00:26:10.702
This version is not good.

00:26:10.702 --> 00:26:12.070
Let's scale it down.

00:26:12.070 --> 00:26:13.050
Let's get rid of it.

00:26:13.050 --> 00:26:14.230
So let's do that.

00:26:14.230 --> 00:26:19.818
Let's kubectl scale fleet
xonotic --replicas=0.

00:26:19.818 --> 00:26:21.360
We're actually going
to make it zero.

00:26:21.360 --> 00:26:22.860
Let's get rid of all of them.

00:26:22.860 --> 00:26:25.680
Now, players-- like I mentioned
before-- players will get mad

00:26:25.680 --> 00:26:27.810
if we kill the games
that they're playing.

00:26:27.810 --> 00:26:29.640
So we don't want that.

00:26:29.640 --> 00:26:33.570
So what Agones does is it's very
aware of that allocated game

00:26:33.570 --> 00:26:34.620
server state.

00:26:34.620 --> 00:26:37.320
So on Fleets themselves we
could do rolling updates.

00:26:37.320 --> 00:26:39.505
We could do scale
up and scale down.

00:26:39.505 --> 00:26:41.130
But no matter what
happens, it's always

00:26:41.130 --> 00:26:43.380
going to say, oh,
that's allocated--

00:26:43.380 --> 00:26:45.880
I'm not going to touch that.

00:26:45.880 --> 00:26:49.350
So now if I look at my
fleet-- there we go.

00:26:49.350 --> 00:26:50.978
We can see we still
have three, right?

00:26:50.978 --> 00:26:52.770
We have those three
allocated game servers.

00:26:52.770 --> 00:26:54.770
We're not going to touch
those until they either

00:26:54.770 --> 00:26:57.180
self shut down or
we specifically

00:26:57.180 --> 00:26:58.600
tell them to be deleted.

00:26:58.600 --> 00:27:00.780
So that's one of the
big powers of Agones

00:27:00.780 --> 00:27:01.863
is managing that workload.

00:27:01.863 --> 00:27:04.405
Everything else on top of that--
all the utilities we provide

00:27:04.405 --> 00:27:06.660
for GameServers-- is super
nice, but this is really

00:27:06.660 --> 00:27:09.483
just the core of
how Agones works.

00:27:09.483 --> 00:27:11.400
Awesome, can we go back
to the slides, please?

00:27:16.740 --> 00:27:17.240
Excellent.

00:27:17.240 --> 00:27:21.652
So wouldn't it be lovely
if we were friends?

00:27:21.652 --> 00:27:23.360
JOSEPH HOLLEY: So
let's talk a little bit

00:27:23.360 --> 00:27:26.810
about putting together
Agones and Open Match.

00:27:26.810 --> 00:27:31.670
So for this session,
we had a matchmaker

00:27:31.670 --> 00:27:34.400
built on Open Match that
understands and coordinates

00:27:34.400 --> 00:27:36.650
Agones Fleets.

00:27:36.650 --> 00:27:39.170
There is customized
matchmaking logic

00:27:39.170 --> 00:27:42.800
that's running within Open Match
to look for compatible players.

00:27:42.800 --> 00:27:45.285
In this case, all
it is looking for

00:27:45.285 --> 00:27:46.660
is that the player
said that they

00:27:46.660 --> 00:27:50.510
wanted to be part of
our GCP Next demo.

00:27:50.510 --> 00:27:53.270
So it's not particularly
exciting matchmaking logic,

00:27:53.270 --> 00:27:55.640
but it does show
that it is possible.

00:27:55.640 --> 00:27:57.470
Once enough players are found--

00:27:57.470 --> 00:28:00.110
in this case, we are looking
for a whopping two players

00:28:00.110 --> 00:28:02.000
who want to play together--

00:28:02.000 --> 00:28:04.640
the matchmaker will
allocate a ready GameServer

00:28:04.640 --> 00:28:05.480
from the fleet.

00:28:05.480 --> 00:28:07.190
And once that's
been allocated, it

00:28:07.190 --> 00:28:10.340
will send back out, through
Open Match, the connection

00:28:10.340 --> 00:28:15.660
details so that Open Match can
connect to the game server.

00:28:15.660 --> 00:28:17.330
So back to the demo.

00:28:20.100 --> 00:28:23.350
Do any of you watch
cooking shows?

00:28:23.350 --> 00:28:24.650
Any-- a couple of you.

00:28:24.650 --> 00:28:26.590
OK, so this may be familiar.

00:28:26.590 --> 00:28:28.980
I have a pre-baked cluster
that I'm going to pull out

00:28:28.980 --> 00:28:32.310
of the oven right now, so that
you don't all have to wait

00:28:32.310 --> 00:28:35.890
for me to spin up a cluster--

00:28:35.890 --> 00:28:37.060
sorry.

00:28:37.060 --> 00:28:40.380
If I ran that command,
you would have to wait.

00:28:40.380 --> 00:28:42.400
Let me switch over to that.

00:28:42.400 --> 00:28:48.760
So basically, this is a
cluster that we made earlier.

00:28:48.760 --> 00:28:51.100
It has plenty of CPUs.

00:28:51.100 --> 00:28:54.040
It already has Agones
installed on it.

00:28:54.040 --> 00:28:55.930
I'll show you briefly
what's running in it.

00:28:59.630 --> 00:29:03.440
So in this case, you
can see it already

00:29:03.440 --> 00:29:04.598
has Open Match installed.

00:29:04.598 --> 00:29:06.890
This all looks really familiar
from the previous demo--

00:29:06.890 --> 00:29:09.500
although this is a different
and slightly larger cluster.

00:29:12.150 --> 00:29:18.470
So first of all, let's
put an Agones Fleet in it.

00:29:18.470 --> 00:29:21.277
So in my case, my fleet
is a little bit different.

00:29:21.277 --> 00:29:22.360
It is not running xonotic.

00:29:22.360 --> 00:29:25.150
It is running
udp-server, which is

00:29:25.150 --> 00:29:28.810
the most ridiculously simple UDP
server we could come up with.

00:29:28.810 --> 00:29:32.440
All it is is a server
that takes UDP clients

00:29:32.440 --> 00:29:34.810
and echoes timestamps to them.

00:29:34.810 --> 00:29:38.680
Once per second, it echoes
the epoch timestamp.

00:29:38.680 --> 00:29:40.780
So it is about as
proof-of-concept

00:29:40.780 --> 00:29:43.060
as you can get.

00:29:43.060 --> 00:29:46.600
Director is the name of
this particular pattern

00:29:46.600 --> 00:29:47.470
of a matchmaker.

00:29:47.470 --> 00:29:49.270
I'm going to stand
one of these up.

00:29:49.270 --> 00:29:52.720
So this matchmaker
understands Agones

00:29:52.720 --> 00:29:56.260
and is looking for a
Fleet called udp-server.

00:29:56.260 --> 00:29:58.450
It is currently
looking at that fleet.

00:29:58.450 --> 00:30:00.550
If I were to show
you logs from it,

00:30:00.550 --> 00:30:02.325
all you would see is
an endless loop of,

00:30:02.325 --> 00:30:04.450
there aren't any players,
there aren't any players,

00:30:04.450 --> 00:30:06.367
there aren't any players,
over and over again.

00:30:06.367 --> 00:30:07.900
So I won't show you that.

00:30:07.900 --> 00:30:10.010
Let me actually put
some players in.

00:30:10.010 --> 00:30:14.020
So the first thing this
does is put one player

00:30:14.020 --> 00:30:16.540
in-- that's significantly
less than the 70,000

00:30:16.540 --> 00:30:17.480
I did last demo.

00:30:17.480 --> 00:30:18.688
But this is going to pay off.

00:30:18.688 --> 00:30:20.660
Just give me a second.

00:30:20.660 --> 00:30:24.200
So we've got one headless
client queued for matching.

00:30:24.200 --> 00:30:27.705
Now I'm going to cue
an interactive client.

00:30:27.705 --> 00:30:28.830
This part can take a while.

00:30:28.830 --> 00:30:29.710
Well, it didn't.

00:30:29.710 --> 00:30:32.620
You can see it already
got an assignment.

00:30:32.620 --> 00:30:35.140
That came from Agones.

00:30:35.140 --> 00:30:38.830
So the matchmaker has
already gone to Agones,

00:30:38.830 --> 00:30:42.130
issued via the Kubernetes
API an allocate request,

00:30:42.130 --> 00:30:44.920
gotten back the allocation
with this IP address

00:30:44.920 --> 00:30:47.440
and the
dynamically-allocated port--

00:30:47.440 --> 00:30:49.780
like Mark showed you earlier--

00:30:49.780 --> 00:30:53.090
that's this
assignment right here.

00:30:53.090 --> 00:30:55.920
And now it's waiting
for me to do something.

00:30:55.920 --> 00:30:59.150
So I'm going to connect
to that UDP server.

00:30:59.150 --> 00:31:02.190
As I said before, this is
not the most exciting demo,

00:31:02.190 --> 00:31:03.170
you'll see at Next.

00:31:03.170 --> 00:31:05.580
But it is a good
proof of concept.

00:31:05.580 --> 00:31:09.060
So if I click Start, I am
telling that UDP server, hey,

00:31:09.060 --> 00:31:10.400
start sending me timestamps.

00:31:10.400 --> 00:31:13.622
So if anyone is curious what
time it is and in UTC right

00:31:13.622 --> 00:31:14.330
now, here you go.

00:31:17.230 --> 00:31:20.620
So we can also stop--
this has a very basic

00:31:20.620 --> 00:31:22.540
[? rebel ?] built in.

00:31:22.540 --> 00:31:26.200
So that unsubscribes me.

00:31:26.200 --> 00:31:27.580
Then I can quit.

00:31:27.580 --> 00:31:31.600
So once I quit, it has
deleted my UDP client.

00:31:31.600 --> 00:31:33.970
That game is still running.

00:31:33.970 --> 00:31:36.070
It still has the
other player on it.

00:31:36.070 --> 00:31:39.370
Now I'm just going to spin
up some headless clients--

00:31:39.370 --> 00:31:43.960
two at a time-- for a
while and watch Agones.

00:31:43.960 --> 00:31:48.670
This is demoing something
that is also really awesome

00:31:48.670 --> 00:31:52.750
that Agones can do for you
that is not in the demo

00:31:52.750 --> 00:31:54.140
that Mark was
showing us earlier,

00:31:54.140 --> 00:31:56.930
which is Agones can
do auto scaling.

00:31:56.930 --> 00:32:02.210
So this is setup with a Fleet
autoscaler policy that says,

00:32:02.210 --> 00:32:08.030
I want at least two available
ready servers at all times.

00:32:08.030 --> 00:32:12.290
So every time that it
allocates all of the servers,

00:32:12.290 --> 00:32:14.740
it will go back to
Kubernetes and start up

00:32:14.740 --> 00:32:17.560
more GameServers for you
to keep that warm buffer he

00:32:17.560 --> 00:32:19.220
was talking about earlier.

00:32:19.220 --> 00:32:22.300
So this is basically just a
bunch numbers on the screen,

00:32:22.300 --> 00:32:24.530
but you can see that
everything is working.

00:32:24.530 --> 00:32:27.550
If I leave this up here while
I talk, you will see that--

00:32:27.550 --> 00:32:28.930
slowly--

00:32:28.930 --> 00:32:34.120
as my matchmaker is
pulling in players,

00:32:34.120 --> 00:32:35.890
allocating servers
from the Fleet,

00:32:35.890 --> 00:32:37.690
and putting players
on those servers,

00:32:37.690 --> 00:32:40.270
the Fleet will then
go behind the scenes

00:32:40.270 --> 00:32:42.160
and start up a couple
of more servers.

00:32:42.160 --> 00:32:44.630
Two at a time is
what I had set for,

00:32:44.630 --> 00:32:46.490
because when I set
it to 20 earlier,

00:32:46.490 --> 00:32:48.700
it just shot up so fast
that you didn't see anything

00:32:48.700 --> 00:32:50.110
in the demo-- but
the number 200,

00:32:50.110 --> 00:32:54.210
like he showed you before--
it wasn't very exciting.

00:32:54.210 --> 00:32:57.720
All right can we go back
to the slides, please?

00:32:57.720 --> 00:32:58.770
Awesome.

00:32:58.770 --> 00:33:01.350
So let's talk about
what that did.

00:33:01.350 --> 00:33:04.650
Here, we're back to
the previous diagram--

00:33:04.650 --> 00:33:08.580
only we've collapsed back
down that giant Open Match box

00:33:08.580 --> 00:33:11.100
to be this nice little
stack in the corner.

00:33:11.100 --> 00:33:13.350
So you can see we had
the players connecting

00:33:13.350 --> 00:33:14.190
to Open Match.

00:33:14.190 --> 00:33:15.750
They connected to
the frontend API

00:33:15.750 --> 00:33:18.050
that we were talking
about before.

00:33:18.050 --> 00:33:21.540
Open Match is running a
matchmaker inside of it

00:33:21.540 --> 00:33:23.840
that understands Kubernetes.

00:33:23.840 --> 00:33:25.560
And that's running
the custom match logic

00:33:25.560 --> 00:33:28.260
I talked about before, looking
for these headless clients that

00:33:28.260 --> 00:33:31.710
are tagged that they want
to play in the next demo.

00:33:31.710 --> 00:33:35.700
As it finds those, it talks
to the Kubernetes API.

00:33:35.700 --> 00:33:39.180
It asks for an allocation from
the fleet that reaches out

00:33:39.180 --> 00:33:40.740
to the Agones controller.

00:33:40.740 --> 00:33:43.170
The Agones controller
goes and grabs

00:33:43.170 --> 00:33:47.100
a particular server, moves
that server to allocated state,

00:33:47.100 --> 00:33:51.060
gets the IP and port
for that server,

00:33:51.060 --> 00:33:56.430
sends that back out Open Match
to the game clients, which--

00:33:56.430 --> 00:33:58.170
it's really hard to see on this.

00:33:58.170 --> 00:34:01.770
But that is a green line, going
back the other way, as opposed

00:34:01.770 --> 00:34:03.670
to the blue line we had before.

00:34:03.670 --> 00:34:07.320
And then those players connect
directly to that dedicated game

00:34:07.320 --> 00:34:09.580
server and play.

00:34:09.580 --> 00:34:13.320
Now, once you've got
all of this in place,

00:34:13.320 --> 00:34:15.639
Open Match is capable
of scaling horizontally.

00:34:15.639 --> 00:34:17.699
If you get a really
big, hit game

00:34:17.699 --> 00:34:20.580
and you need to handle a much
larger player base than you

00:34:20.580 --> 00:34:24.360
expected, Agones is capable
of handling basic auto scaling

00:34:24.360 --> 00:34:25.540
for you.

00:34:25.540 --> 00:34:27.923
So this can
significantly cut down

00:34:27.923 --> 00:34:29.340
the amount of
development time you

00:34:29.340 --> 00:34:32.370
need to do around building
the nuts and bolts

00:34:32.370 --> 00:34:35.650
part of your game backend.

00:34:35.650 --> 00:34:38.010
So, Mark, do you want
to talk a little bit

00:34:38.010 --> 00:34:39.179
about other features?

00:34:39.179 --> 00:34:42.732
MARK MANDEL: Sweet, so we
showed a bunch of stuff today.

00:34:42.732 --> 00:34:44.190
There's a lot of
work that has gone

00:34:44.190 --> 00:34:45.472
into both of these projects.

00:34:45.472 --> 00:34:47.639
So there's a variety of
stuff that we haven't really

00:34:47.639 --> 00:34:48.747
talked about.

00:34:48.747 --> 00:34:50.414
One of which, I want
to highlight again.

00:34:50.414 --> 00:34:52.350
We sort of touched on
it previously-- both

00:34:52.350 --> 00:34:54.417
of these projects work
across cloud providers.

00:34:54.417 --> 00:34:56.250
There's nothing special
about the Kubernetes

00:34:56.250 --> 00:34:57.960
that we're running
on, nothing like that.

00:34:57.960 --> 00:35:00.630
It all uses standard Kubernetes
tooling across the board

00:35:00.630 --> 00:35:02.415
and both of them.

00:35:02.415 --> 00:35:04.790
Joseph did touch on this, but
there is fleet, deployment,

00:35:04.790 --> 00:35:05.970
and node autoscaling.

00:35:05.970 --> 00:35:08.010
Basically both projects
have the capability

00:35:08.010 --> 00:35:09.773
to autoscale with your load.

00:35:09.773 --> 00:35:11.190
So if you're running
in the cloud,

00:35:11.190 --> 00:35:13.740
you have that elastic
ability to shrink and grow

00:35:13.740 --> 00:35:15.780
as your requirements need.

00:35:15.780 --> 00:35:17.905
Both projects have
local development tools.

00:35:17.905 --> 00:35:20.280
So if you want your developers
to not necessarily spin up

00:35:20.280 --> 00:35:22.212
an entire Kubernetes
cluster locally--

00:35:22.212 --> 00:35:24.420
or they just want a lightweight
testing environment--

00:35:24.420 --> 00:35:26.572
they both have tools
available to do that.

00:35:26.572 --> 00:35:28.530
I showed off some of the
metrics and dashboards

00:35:28.530 --> 00:35:29.738
that are available in Agones.

00:35:29.738 --> 00:35:31.267
There's a whole lot
more, as well as

00:35:31.267 --> 00:35:33.600
metrics and dashboards that
are available for Open Match

00:35:33.600 --> 00:35:34.020
as well.

00:35:34.020 --> 00:35:36.145
JOSEPH HOLLEY: Yeah, we
use all the same standards.

00:35:36.145 --> 00:35:38.900
We also use OpenCensus,
Prometheus, and Grafana.

00:35:38.900 --> 00:35:42.145
MARK MANDEL: Yep, So
it's all good stuff.

00:35:42.145 --> 00:35:44.520
Future developments-- I'll
talk a little bit about a road

00:35:44.520 --> 00:35:46.230
map for Agones.

00:35:46.230 --> 00:35:48.160
We're looking to
hit 1.0 this year.

00:35:48.160 --> 00:35:49.410
That's a big, broad statement.

00:35:49.410 --> 00:35:51.360
That gives us lots
of room to wiggle.

00:35:51.360 --> 00:35:53.992
I'm happy with
that, which is good.

00:35:53.992 --> 00:35:55.200
But we have a lot more stuff.

00:35:55.200 --> 00:35:56.940
We've been doing a lot of
performance improvements

00:35:56.940 --> 00:35:58.137
in the last few releases.

00:35:58.137 --> 00:35:59.970
But many more [? are ?]
[? still ?] to come.

00:35:59.970 --> 00:36:01.770
We want to do more metric
collection display.

00:36:01.770 --> 00:36:03.812
There's a wide variety of
metrics for GameServers

00:36:03.812 --> 00:36:07.828
that we want to take advantage
of and display for customers.

00:36:07.828 --> 00:36:09.870
Better commercial engine
integration-- right now,

00:36:09.870 --> 00:36:11.910
there's lots of people who
are using Unreal and Unity.

00:36:11.910 --> 00:36:13.380
But we want to have some
standardized tooling

00:36:13.380 --> 00:36:15.810
around that, just to make
life easier for people.

00:36:15.810 --> 00:36:18.293
Multi cluster and multi
cluster coordination--

00:36:18.293 --> 00:36:19.710
we're looking at
a variety of ways

00:36:19.710 --> 00:36:23.020
on how we can do that and make
that easier for people as well.

00:36:23.020 --> 00:36:25.540
And it looks like in 1.14, we
have some really nice Windows

00:36:25.540 --> 00:36:26.040
hosting.

00:36:26.040 --> 00:36:27.810
So we want to look at how
we can support that for game

00:36:27.810 --> 00:36:29.852
developers, especially at
development time, where

00:36:29.852 --> 00:36:31.740
a lot of game
developers use Windows,

00:36:31.740 --> 00:36:34.583
but also supporting it and
production workloads as well.

00:36:34.583 --> 00:36:36.000
Do you want talk
about Open Match?

00:36:36.000 --> 00:36:38.790
JOSEPH HOLLEY: Yeah, so from
the Open Match standpoint,

00:36:38.790 --> 00:36:41.090
Unity has recently
contributed their design

00:36:41.090 --> 00:36:44.610
for a longer-lived matching
logic, which is to say,

00:36:44.610 --> 00:36:47.100
you can stand up the
matching logic in Kubernetes

00:36:47.100 --> 00:36:50.850
and not spin it down in between
the cycles of your matchmakers.

00:36:50.850 --> 00:36:54.480
So it makes it very fast to
kick off the matchmaking logic.

00:36:54.480 --> 00:36:56.310
It's already there and running.

00:36:56.310 --> 00:36:59.130
It'll just spin down
and use very little CPU

00:36:59.130 --> 00:37:01.440
whenever it's not necessary.

00:37:01.440 --> 00:37:03.300
We're also expanding
metrics and dashboards.

00:37:03.300 --> 00:37:05.700
We're actually in the process
of doing another design

00:37:05.700 --> 00:37:09.570
iteration on what metrics people
want to get out of Open Match.

00:37:09.570 --> 00:37:12.420
So if you're interested
in that, please come

00:37:12.420 --> 00:37:16.290
put your comments in the issues
on the GitHub repository.

00:37:16.290 --> 00:37:19.950
We are going to be implementing
player and match tracings, so

00:37:19.950 --> 00:37:22.530
that you will be able
to pretty easily see

00:37:22.530 --> 00:37:25.660
when a player went into your
system, what exactly they did.

00:37:25.660 --> 00:37:27.820
What were all the processes
they went through?

00:37:27.820 --> 00:37:30.840
Which match making
functions considered them?

00:37:30.840 --> 00:37:32.100
Which match they got put in?

00:37:32.100 --> 00:37:33.600
Which server they ended up on?

00:37:33.600 --> 00:37:35.058
Those should all
be things that you

00:37:35.058 --> 00:37:37.400
should be able to trace
through whenever you needed

00:37:37.400 --> 00:37:39.763
to debug something, or
troubleshoot something, or just

00:37:39.763 --> 00:37:41.430
validate that things
are working the way

00:37:41.430 --> 00:37:42.550
that you expect them to.

00:37:42.550 --> 00:37:45.510
We want that to all be
built-in and baked-in tools

00:37:45.510 --> 00:37:48.000
that we can all
work towards taking

00:37:48.000 --> 00:37:50.430
for granted in matchmaking.

00:37:50.430 --> 00:37:52.560
And finally, we're
